Subject: How to handle known removals of unmarked animals
Message-ID: <xZQFQT32Y1o.3_pyXSXmDQAJ@secrgroup.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/secrgroup/c/xZQFQT32Y1o/m/3_pyXSXmDQAJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

We are conducting a population estimate of wolves in Alaska using DNA-based capture-recapture methods. This past season was our 5th year and, as it turns out, the hunting/trapping season was shifted to earlier in the season and therefore coincided with our study period. Approximately 45 animals were removed by trapping or hunting from our study area during DNA sampling. Approximately 15 of them were ones that had been previously detected in our hair snares and harvest took place within 1 sampling occasion from the last detection. In those cases, it seems relatively straightforward to be able to mark those identified animals as a known removal (i.e., treat them as being removed at the last sampling occasion). However, that leaves around 30 animals that were known to have been removed but had not been previously identified. To complicate matters, hunter/trapper reporting of harvest location is not very precise, with location usually reported only to the scale of watershed, peninsula, or other large geographical feature. Timing of those harvests are only known to within a 2-week period. These removals potentially represent a very large proportion of the total number of animals in the study area (up to 30% of the previous year's population estimate). Is there something I can do to include these unmarked removals in the analysis?

