From u118cea91a1619106 at groups.google.com  Tue Aug 21 16:37:13 2018
From: u118cea91a1619106 at groups.google.com (Murray Efford)
Date: Tue, 21 Aug 2018 16:37:13 +0000
Subject: Re: Estimating N when groups are used
Message-ID: <cTn-tb8If-g.efU_2hKSEQAJ@secrgroup.groups.google.com>
In-Reply-To: <cTn-tb8If-g.fjuWZkaPAAAJ@secrgroup.groups.google.com>
References: <cTn-tb8If-g.fjuWZkaPAAAJ@secrgroup.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/secrgroup/c/cTn-tb8If-g/m/efU_2hKSEQAJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

It seems that a discrepancy between E.N and R.N arises in 'secr' with models that include individual variation in detection (both my earlier examples fit2 and fit3 are of this type). The code only considered the detection probability (and effective sampling area esa) of the first animal. The problem has been there forever. The sensible path is to take the harmonic mean of the individual esa as is done when computing H-T estimates of density in derived(). I have made this 1-line change to the internal function sumDpdot that is called by region.N() and re-built the package. This passes routine checks but won't be on CRAN until later in September (I'm traveling and CRAN incoming has a long maintenance shut down in early Sept.). Meantime you can access it at https://www.otago.ac.nz/density/zip/secr_3.1.7.zip (Windows binary) or https://www.otago.ac.nz/density/zip/secr_3.1.7.tar.gz (source). <https://www.otago.ac.nz/density/zip/secr_3.1.7.tar.gz>

Repeating the earlier examples
region.N(fit2)
#     estimate SE.estimate      lcl      ucl  n
# E.N 81.20962    30.82438 39.56661 166.6810 20
# R.N 81.20962    29.47767 45.00768 169.8187 20
region.N(fit3)
#     estimate SE.estimate      lcl      ucl  n
# E.N 81.21036    31.97651 38.58809 170.9108 20
# R.N 81.21037    30.68040 44.20184 174.8109 20

E.N and R.N now agree.

I'm guilty of not attending to parts of 'secr' that never much excited me (N-hat and telemetry) - I'm sorry if this has tripped people up.

Murray

